{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cf5fd0ad\" lines=\"10-22\">\n<description>\nBuild a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square grid treated as one complete sprite.\n\nRule\n- Each output quadrant is a 2x2 repetition of a rotated copy of the whole input.\n- The quadrant rotations are upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees\n  clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, and lower-right unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is a 4x4 grid of original-size blocks, not a per-pixel scale-up.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"0934a4d8\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReveal the rectangular patch hidden by the solid marker using the grid's reflection symmetries.\n\nInput structure:\n- A square colored pattern with one solid rectangular marker color occluding a patch.\n- The visible pattern has horizontal and vertical reflection symmetry on a large lower/right strip.\n- The top and left outer strips also expose transpose counterparts for cells not recoverable by those reflections.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique color whose full extent is a solid rectangle; this rectangle is the output region.\n- Infer the reflected strip start as the smallest row/column offset whose lower/right strips are mirror-consistent, ignoring marker pixels.\n- For each marker pixel, copy the unique non-marker value in its horizontal/vertical reflection orbit; if that orbit is entirely hidden in the outer strip, use the transposed top/left-strip counterpart.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not part of the pattern; it covers a missing rectangle whose cells are still determined by the visible mirror or transpose copies elsewhere in the grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"278e5215\" lines=\"51-66\">\n<description>\nRecolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.\n- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the mask object's bbox.\n- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.\n- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n<description>\nReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n\nRule:\n- Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n- Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n- In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n\nKey insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"48634b99\" lines=\"14-27\">\n<description>\nAdvance the marker from one half of a vertical bar to the same half of the next longer bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms many vertical one-cell-wide bars; the other appears as a single vertical marker segment.\n- The marker segment occupies exactly the top or bottom half of its containing vertical bar.\nRule\n- Recolor the current marker segment back to the bar color.\n- Find the unique all-bar-color vertical bar whose height is two pixels greater than the marked source bar.\n- Recolor the same half of that next longer bar to the marker color.\nKey insight\n- Each bar's total height encodes an ordered size, and the marker advances from a 2k bar to the corresponding 2(k+1) bar while preserving whether it marks the top or bottom half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"522fdd07\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- Keep each square's color and center.\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\" lines=\"71-91\">\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"18447a8d\" lines=\"33-48\">\n<description>\nMove each right-side colored shape into the left slot whose template color is its exact complement.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has two equal-width slots separated by one background column.\n- Non-background rows form horizontal bands separated by all-background rows.\n- Each band has a shared template color in the left slot and one colored source shape in the right slot.\n\nRule:\n- For each band, define its destination shape as the cells in the left slot not occupied by the template color.\n- Normalize every right-side source shape to right-slot coordinates and match it to the unique equal destination shape.\n- Clear the original right-side source pixels and paint the matched left-slot complement with the source color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe right-side objects are not placed by row order; each one completes the unique left template whose missing cells have the same shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d749d46f\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nPack each solid foreground rectangle twice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\nseparate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n\nRule\nBuild a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\nversions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\nrectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\nconsecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\nbottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\nagain separated by one background column.\n\nKey insight\nThe input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\ncontributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\npreserving left-to-right object order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"902510d5\" lines=\"58-80\">\n<description>\nKeep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the\n  corner from which the triangle is painted.\n- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the\n  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.\n- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.\n- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two\n  non-corner control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.\n- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.\n- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
